Author: lora-dataset-studio <noreply@lora-dataset-studio.dev>
Date:   Mon Aug 10 11:23:32 2026 +0200

    fix(bank): stop flagging bokeh portraits as blurry
    
    Reported through community feedback: the 🌫 blurry flag caught shots with a
    razor-sharp subject on a creamy out-of-focus background — technically true of
    the frame as a whole, and exactly backwards for curation, since those are
    often the best images in a bank.
    
    Cause: sharpness was the variance of the Laplacian over the WHOLE analysis
    copy, so a large soft background averaged the sharp subject away and dragged
    the score under bank.sharpness_min (100).
    
    The Laplacian is still recovered from the same two clamp-free half-
    convolutions, and they still run ONCE on the whole frame — filtering each
    tile separately would invent an edge at every tile border. The E[L²] identity
    is per-pixel, so cropping the two filtered images yields exact per-tile
    moments from their histograms, for free. The frame is split into at most 8×8
    tiles (never below 32 px a side, so small images degrade to a single tile and
    keep the historical whole-frame score) and blur_score is the 90th percentile
    of the tile variances — not the max, which one JPEG-artefact block or burnt
    highlight would use to certify a soft image as sharp.
    
    Threshold semantics are unchanged and still applied at read time: "even the
    sharpest region of this image is soft". Scores land on a new, higher scale;
    already-scanned banks pick them up at their next Quality re-scan, and the
    settings reference says so.
    
    Measured on the synthetic fixtures: bokeh shot 78 (flagged) → 323 (kept);
    uniformly blurred copy 2.2 → 2.5 (still flagged); tiny full-contrast speck in
    a soft frame 869 (wrongly kept) → 2.5 (now flagged).
